2026-11-10  G. Branden Robinson <g.branden.robinson@gmail.com>

	* src/preproc/tbl/main.cpp (process_data): Fix code style nit.
	Arrange equality comparison to avoid inadvertent assignment to
	lvalue.

2026-11-09  G. Branden Robinson <g.branden.robinson@gmail.com>

	* src/preproc/tbl/main.cpp (process_format): Stop deleting the
	`format` passed in as an argument if rejecting table description
	because its final row definition contains only horizontal rules.
	I can't improve on Dickson Wu's analysis (slightly recast).
	  "In src/preproc/tbl/main.cpp (1.25.0), `process_table()` owns
	  the first format ... [Upon] `.T&`, `process_data()` hands that
	  same object back to `process_format()` as `current_format`
	  {line 1617}, and `process_format()` keeps extending it in
	  place (`f = current_format;`, line 1254).  The all-lines check
	  then deletes it ... `process_data()` gives up and returns [a]
	  null [pointer], and `process_table()` reaches `delete fmt`
	  with a dangling pointer.  The other continued-format error
	  path, "cannot increase the number of columns", already returns
	  without deleting.  Only this one gets ownership wrong."
	Remedy by deleting the deletion of `f` on the "all-lines" error
	path.  For me, using a GCC 12 ASAN build, this resolves the
	heap-use-after-free and does not introduce a memory leak.

	Fixes <https://savannah.gnu.org/bugs/?68897>.  Problem appears
	to date back to groff's birth.  Thanks to Dickson Wu for the
	report; a cogent analysis; and a plausible, cautious recommended
	fix.  I went with a different one, doing nothing with `f` on the
	error path.

